# Skill: Fix -Werror=unused-parameter in C/C++
Bug Fix Request: Unused Parameter in C++ Function

1. Environment & Context

Project: Generic C++ Project

File Path: path/to/your/file.cpp

Compiler Flag causing issue: -Werror=unused-parameter

2. Error Log

path/to/your/file.cpp:15:45: error: unused parameter 'unusedStr' [-Werror=unused-parameter]
 void MyClass::processData(int data, const std::string &unusedStr)


3. Original Code (Current Implementation)

Around line 15 in MyClass.cpp:

void MyClass::processData(int data, const std::string &unusedStr) 
{
    // Only 'data' is used. 'unusedStr' is not used.
    std::cout << "Data: " << data << std::endl;
}


4. Proposed Fixes (Solutions with General Templates)

The following three methods resolve the unused parameter error under the -Werror=unused-parameter flag. They are presented as General Templates followed by an application example to help AI models easily map the pattern to any function.

Option 1: Commenting out the unused parameter name (Recommended for pure C++)

This is the cleanest approach when the parameter is required by an interface but not used in the implementation.

General Template:

// Before:
ReturnType FunctionName(ParameterType parameterName) { ... }

// After:
ReturnType FunctionName(ParameterType /*parameterName*/) { ... }


Applied to this issue:

// Suppress warning by commenting out the parameter name
void MyClass::processData(int data, const std::string &/*unusedStr*/) 
{
    std::cout << "Data: " << data << std::endl;
}


Option 2: Casting to void (Traditional C/C++ Approach)

An alternative approach often used in legacy codebases or when the parameter might be used in future revisions.

General Template:

// Before:
ReturnType FunctionName(ParameterType parameterName) 
{
    // parameterName is not used
}

// After:
ReturnType FunctionName(ParameterType parameterName) 
{
    (void)parameterName; // Explicitly silence the compiler warning
}


Applied to this issue:

void MyClass::processData(int data, const std::string &unusedStr) 
{
    (void)unusedStr; // Cast to void to suppress unused-parameter warning
    std::cout << "Data: " << data << std::endl;
}


Option 3: Using [[maybe_unused]] attribute (Modern C++17 or newer)

The modern C++ standard approach to signal to the compiler that a parameter might purposefully go unused.

General Template:

// Before:
ReturnType FunctionName(ParameterType parameterName) { ... }

// After:
ReturnType FunctionName([[maybe_unused]] ParameterType parameterName) { ... }


Applied to this issue:

void MyClass::processData(int data, [[maybe_unused]] const std::string &unusedStr) 
{
    std::cout << "Data: " << data << std::endl;
}


5. Unified Diff (For Option 1 - Recommended)

diff --git a/src/utils/MyClass.cpp b/src/utils/MyClass.cpp
--- a/src/utils/MyClass.cpp
+++ b/src/utils/MyClass.cpp
@@ -15,3 +15,3 @@
-void MyClass::processData(int data, const std::string &unusedStr)
+void MyClass::processData(int data, const std::string &/*unusedStr*/)
 {
 }